            <ArticleTitle>A Novel Type of Coracobrachialis Muscle Variation and a Proposed New Classification.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e1466</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7759/cureus.1466</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>During a routine anatomical dissection in the right brachium of a 75-year-old male cadaver, a novel variation of the coracobrachialis muscle (CB) was discovered. It consisted of three parts: proximal, starting from the upper border of the scapula; medial, presenting the well-accessed CB; and distal, formed by a proximal tendinous part that arose from the coracoid process and then transitioned into a well-defined muscle body and a distal tendinous part attached to the medial epicondyle. We also present a new systematic classification of CB variations, dividing them into two simple groups.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
